{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"segment_tree","title":"Segment Tree","group":"Advanced Structures","order":22,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A segment tree answers <strong>range queries</strong> (sum, min, max, …) over an array and <strong>updates</strong> single elements, both in <code>O(log n)</code>. It is the structure to use when both operations are frequent, because a plain array makes queries slow and a prefix-sum array makes updates slow.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Segment tree</strong> — a binary tree over a fixed-size array in which every node stores an aggregate (e.g. the sum) of one contiguous segment: the root covers the whole array, each child covers one half of its parent's segment, and the leaves are the individual elements.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Segment tree of sums over [10, 20, 30, 40], stored in an array like a heap</figcaption><pre><code>arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]            index:  0   1   2   3\n\n                [0..3] = 100                 tree[0]\n               /            \\\n      [0..1] = 30          [2..3] = 70        tree[1]  tree[2]\n      /       \\            /       \\\n[0]=10     [1]=20    [2]=30     [3]=40        tree[3] tree[4] tree[5] tree[6]\n\ntree array:  100  30  70  10  20  30  40\n              0    1   2   3   4   5   6        children of i are 2i+1 and 2i+2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]; getSum(0, 2); getSum(1, 3); update(1, 60); getSum(0, 2)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>60, 90, then 100</code></p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scale\"></i>Why not a plain array or prefix sums?</h3>\n<p>Suppose <code>x</code> operations arrive, of which a quarter are range-sum queries and three quarters are updates. With a plain array queries cost <code>O(n)</code> and updates <code>O(1)</code>, so the average is <code>(x/4 · O(n) + 3x/4 · O(1)) / x = O(n)</code>. With a prefix-sum array it is the reverse — queries <code>O(1)</code>, updates <code>O(n)</code> — and the average is again <code>O(n)</code>. A segment tree makes <strong>both</strong> <code>O(log n)</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Range sum</th><th>Point update</th><th>Preprocessing</th><th>Space</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plain array</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>—</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Prefix-sum array</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Segment tree</strong></td><td><strong>O(log n)</strong></td><td><strong>O(log n)</strong></td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n) (≈ 4n slots)</td></tr><tr><td>Binary indexed tree</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>O(n log n) (O(n) possible)</td><td>O(n) (n + 1 slots)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Segment trees work for any <strong>associative</strong> aggregate: sum, min, max, gcd, XOR, number of zeros, … Replace the <code>+</code> in the three functions below and nothing else changes. They also extend to range updates with lazy propagation — the binary indexed tree does not handle min/max.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-ruler\"></i>Size of the tree array</h3>\n<p>Children of node <code>i</code> live at <code>2i + 1</code> and <code>2i + 2</code>, so the tree is stored in a plain array with no pointers. How large must it be?</p>\n<ul><li>If <code>n</code> is a power of two the tree is perfect: <code>2n − 1</code> nodes.</li><li>Otherwise let <code>x</code> be the smallest power of two <strong>greater than</strong> <code>n</code>; the tree needs at most <code>2x − 1</code> slots (some stay unused).</li><li>In practice allocate <code>4n</code> — it is always enough (<code>2x − 1 &lt; 4n</code>) and saves computing <code>x</code>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"formula\">size = 2 · 2^⌈log₂ n⌉ − 1 (≤ 4n)</div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><code>n = 5</code> → <code>x = 8</code> → at most <code>15</code> slots; <code>n = 4</code> → exactly <code>7</code> slots, all used.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hammer\"></i>Constructing the segment tree</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>tree = [100, 30, 70, 10, 20, 30, 40]</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Build recursively: if the segment is a single element store it; otherwise build the left half and the right half, then store their sum. Every node is written exactly once — <code>O(n)</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>build(ss, se, si)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>ss</code>/<code>se</code> = segment start/end in <code>arr</code>, <code>si</code> = index of this node in <code>tree</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Leaf</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>ss == se</code> → <code>tree[si] = arr[ss]</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Internal node</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>mid = (ss + se) / 2</code>; <code>tree[si] = build(ss, mid, 2si+1) + build(mid+1, se, 2si+2)</code>.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def build(arr, tree, ss, se, si):\n    if ss == se:                                        # leaf: a single element\n        tree[si] = arr[ss]\n        return tree[si]\n    mid = (ss + se) // 2\n    tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2)\n    return tree[si]                                     # parent = left sum + right sum\n\ndef tree_size(n):\n    x = 1\n    while x &lt; n:                                        # smallest power of two ≥ n\n        x *= 2\n    return 2 * x - 1\n\narr = [10, 20, 30, 40]\ntree = [0] * tree_size(len(arr))\nbuild(arr, tree, 0, len(arr) - 1, 0)\nprint(\"tree:\", \" \".join(map(str, tree)))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint build(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; arr, vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; tree, int ss, int se, int si) {\n    if (ss == se) return tree[si] = arr[ss];            // leaf: a single element\n    int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n    return tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1)\n                    + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);   // parent = left sum + right sum\n}\n\nint treeSize(int n) {\n    int x = 1;\n    while (x &lt; n) x *= 2;                                // smallest power of two ≥ n\n    return 2 * x - 1;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; arr = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    int n = arr.size();\n    vector&lt;int&gt; tree(treeSize(n), 0);\n    build(arr, tree, 0, n - 1, 0);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"tree:\";\n    for (int v : tree) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; v;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int build(int[] arr, int[] tree, int ss, int se, int si) {\n        if (ss == se) return tree[si] = arr[ss];        // leaf: a single element\n        int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n        return tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1)\n                        + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);   // parent = left sum + right sum\n    }\n\n    static int treeSize(int n) {\n        int x = 1;\n        while (x &lt; n) x *= 2;                            // smallest power of two ≥ n\n        return 2 * x - 1;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n        int[] tree = new int[treeSize(arr.length)];\n        build(arr, tree, 0, arr.length - 1, 0);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"tree:\");\n        for (int v : tree) sb.append(' ').append(v);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function build(arr, tree, ss, se, si) {\n    if (ss === se) return (tree[si] = arr[ss]);         // leaf: a single element\n    const mid = Math.floor((ss + se) / 2);\n    return (tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1)\n                     + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2));   // parent = left sum + right sum\n}\n\nfunction treeSize(n) {\n    let x = 1;\n    while (x &lt; n) x *= 2;                                // smallest power of two ≥ n\n    return 2 * x - 1;\n}\n\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30, 40];\nconst tree = new Array(treeSize(arr.length)).fill(0);\nbuild(arr, tree, 0, arr.length - 1, 0);\nconsole.log(\"tree:\", tree.join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>tree: 100 30 70 10 20 30 40</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) — each of the ≈ 2n nodes is computed once</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Range query</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]; getSum(0, 2)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>60</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>getSum(1, 3)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>90</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Walk down from the root. A node's segment relates to the query range <code>[qs, qe]</code> in one of three ways: <strong>completely inside</strong> → return its stored sum without going deeper; <strong>completely outside</strong> → contribute <code>0</code>; <strong>partially overlapping</strong> → recurse into both children and add.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>The three cases — at most two nodes per level are partially overlapped, so the query touches O(log n) nodes</figcaption><pre><code>getSum(1, 2) on [10, 20, 30, 40]\n\n         [0..3]  partial overlap → recurse\n        /      \\\n  [0..1] partial  [2..3] partial\n  /    \\          /    \\\n[0]    [1]      [2]    [3]\noutside inside  inside outside\n  0     20       30      0        → 20 + 30 = 50</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def build(arr, tree, ss, se, si):\n    if ss == se:\n        tree[si] = arr[ss]\n        return tree[si]\n    mid = (ss + se) // 2\n    tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2)\n    return tree[si]\n\ndef get_sum_rec(tree, qs, qe, ss, se, si):\n    if qe &lt; ss or qs &gt; se:                              # segment completely outside the query\n        return 0\n    if qs &lt;= ss and se &lt;= qe:                           # segment completely inside the query\n        return tree[si]\n    mid = (ss + se) // 2                                # partial overlap: ask both children\n    return get_sum_rec(tree, qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + get_sum_rec(tree, qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2)\n\ndef get_sum(tree, n, qs, qe):\n    if qs &lt; 0 or qe &gt; n - 1 or qs &gt; qe:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid range\")\n    return get_sum_rec(tree, qs, qe, 0, n - 1, 0)\n\narr = [10, 20, 30, 40]\nn = len(arr)\nx = 1\nwhile x &lt; n:\n    x *= 2\ntree = [0] * (2 * x - 1)\nbuild(arr, tree, 0, n - 1, 0)\nprint(\"tree:\", \" \".join(map(str, tree)))\nfor qs, qe in [(0, 2), (1, 3), (2, 2)]:\n    print(f\"sum({qs}, {qe}):\", get_sum(tree, n, qs, qe))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint build(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; arr, vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; tree, int ss, int se, int si) {\n    if (ss == se) return tree[si] = arr[ss];\n    int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n    return tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n}\n\nint getSumRec(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; tree, int qs, int qe, int ss, int se, int si) {\n    if (qe &lt; ss || qs &gt; se) return 0;                   // segment completely outside the query\n    if (qs &lt;= ss &amp;&amp; se &lt;= qe) return tree[si];          // segment completely inside the query\n    int mid = (ss + se) / 2;                             // partial overlap: ask both children\n    return getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n}\n\nint getSum(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; tree, int n, int qs, int qe) {\n    if (qs &lt; 0 || qe &gt; n - 1 || qs &gt; qe) throw invalid_argument(\"invalid range\");\n    return getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, 0, n - 1, 0);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; arr = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    int n = arr.size(), x = 1;\n    while (x &lt; n) x *= 2;\n    vector&lt;int&gt; tree(2 * x - 1, 0);\n    build(arr, tree, 0, n - 1, 0);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"tree:\";\n    for (int v : tree) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; v;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    int queries[3][2] = {{0, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 2}};\n    for (auto&amp; q : queries) cout &lt;&lt; \"sum(\" &lt;&lt; q[0] &lt;&lt; \", \" &lt;&lt; q[1] &lt;&lt; \"): \" &lt;&lt; getSum(tree, n, q[0], q[1]) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static int build(int[] arr, int[] tree, int ss, int se, int si) {\n        if (ss == se) return tree[si] = arr[ss];\n        int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n        return tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n    }\n\n    static int getSumRec(int[] tree, int qs, int qe, int ss, int se, int si) {\n        if (qe &lt; ss || qs &gt; se) return 0;               // segment completely outside the query\n        if (qs &lt;= ss &amp;&amp; se &lt;= qe) return tree[si];      // segment completely inside the query\n        int mid = (ss + se) / 2;                         // partial overlap: ask both children\n        return getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n    }\n\n    static int getSum(int[] tree, int n, int qs, int qe) {\n        if (qs &lt; 0 || qe &gt; n - 1 || qs &gt; qe) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"invalid range\");\n        return getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, 0, n - 1, 0);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n        int n = arr.length, x = 1;\n        while (x &lt; n) x *= 2;\n        int[] tree = new int[2 * x - 1];\n        build(arr, tree, 0, n - 1, 0);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"tree:\");\n        for (int v : tree) sb.append(' ').append(v);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        int[][] queries = {{0, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 2}};\n        for (int[] q : queries) System.out.println(\"sum(\" + q[0] + \", \" + q[1] + \"): \" + getSum(tree, n, q[0], q[1]));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function build(arr, tree, ss, se, si) {\n    if (ss === se) return (tree[si] = arr[ss]);\n    const mid = Math.floor((ss + se) / 2);\n    return (tree[si] = build(arr, tree, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + build(arr, tree, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2));\n}\n\nfunction getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, ss, se, si) {\n    if (qe &lt; ss || qs &gt; se) return 0;                   // segment completely outside the query\n    if (qs &lt;= ss &amp;&amp; se &lt;= qe) return tree[si];          // segment completely inside the query\n    const mid = Math.floor((ss + se) / 2);               // partial overlap: ask both children\n    return getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n}\n\nfunction getSum(tree, n, qs, qe) {\n    if (qs &lt; 0 || qe &gt; n - 1 || qs &gt; qe) throw new Error(\"invalid range\");\n    return getSumRec(tree, qs, qe, 0, n - 1, 0);\n}\n\nconst arr = [10, 20, 30, 40];\nconst n = arr.length;\nlet x = 1;\nwhile (x &lt; n) x *= 2;\nconst tree = new Array(2 * x - 1).fill(0);\nbuild(arr, tree, 0, n - 1, 0);\nconsole.log(\"tree:\", tree.join(\" \"));\nfor (const [qs, qe] of [[0, 2], [1, 3], [2, 2]]) console.log(`sum(${qs}, ${qe}):`, getSum(tree, n, qs, qe));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>tree: 100 30 70 10 20 30 40\nsum(0, 2): 60\nsum(1, 3): 90\nsum(2, 2): 30</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(log n) per query</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(log n) recursion depth</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>The order of the two base cases matters: test <em>completely outside</em> before <em>completely inside</em>. For a single-element segment both <code>qe &lt; ss</code> and <code>qs &lt;= ss &amp;&amp; se &lt;= qe</code> can be evaluated — the outside test must win when the leaf is not in the range.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>Point update</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 20, 30, 40]; update(1, 60); getSum(0, 2)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>100 (the tree becomes 140 70 70 10 60 30 40)</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Only the nodes whose segment <strong>contains index <code>i</code></strong> — one per level, root to leaf — are affected. Compute <code>diff = newValue − arr[i]</code>, then walk down from the root adding <code>diff</code> to every node on the path to leaf <code>i</code> and ignoring the subtrees that do not contain <code>i</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Compute the difference</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>diff = new_val − arr[i]</code>; also store <code>arr[i] = new_val</code> so later updates compute the right difference.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>update(ss, se, i, si, diff)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">If <code>i</code> is outside <code>[ss, se]</code> → return (this subtree is untouched).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Add and descend</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>tree[si] += diff</code>; if the segment has more than one element, recurse into both children (only the one containing <code>i</code> does any work).</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Exactly one node per level changes</figcaption><pre><code>update(1, 60) on [10, 20, 30, 40]: diff = 60 − 20 = +40\n\n         [0..3] 100 → 140            ← contains index 1: += 40\n        /            \\\n  [0..1] 30 → 70      [2..3] 70       ← index 1 not in [2..3]: skip\n  /       \\\n[0] 10    [1] 20 → 60                 ← the leaf itself</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class SegmentTree:\n    def __init__(self, arr):\n        self.arr = arr[:]\n        self.n = len(arr)\n        x = 1\n        while x &lt; self.n:\n            x *= 2\n        self.tree = [0] * (2 * x - 1)\n        self._build(0, self.n - 1, 0)\n\n    def _build(self, ss, se, si):\n        if ss == se:\n            self.tree[si] = self.arr[ss]\n            return self.tree[si]\n        mid = (ss + se) // 2\n        self.tree[si] = self._build(ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + self._build(mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2)\n        return self.tree[si]\n\n    def get_sum(self, qs, qe, ss=0, se=None, si=0):\n        if se is None:\n            se = self.n - 1\n        if qe &lt; ss or qs &gt; se:\n            return 0\n        if qs &lt;= ss and se &lt;= qe:\n            return self.tree[si]\n        mid = (ss + se) // 2\n        return self.get_sum(qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + self.get_sum(qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2)\n\n    def update(self, i, new_val):\n        diff = new_val - self.arr[i]                    # how much every ancestor must change\n        self.arr[i] = new_val\n        self._update(0, self.n - 1, i, 0, diff)\n\n    def _update(self, ss, se, i, si, diff):\n        if i &lt; ss or i &gt; se:                            # index not in this segment\n            return\n        self.tree[si] += diff                           # this node's segment contains i\n        if ss &lt; se:\n            mid = (ss + se) // 2\n            self._update(ss, mid, i, 2 * si + 1, diff)\n            self._update(mid + 1, se, i, 2 * si + 2, diff)\n\nst = SegmentTree([10, 20, 30, 40])\nprint(\"tree:\", \" \".join(map(str, st.tree)))\nprint(\"sum(0, 2):\", st.get_sum(0, 2))\nprint(\"update(1, 60)\")\nst.update(1, 60)\nprint(\"tree:\", \" \".join(map(str, st.tree)))\nprint(\"sum(0, 2):\", st.get_sum(0, 2))\nprint(\"sum(1, 3):\", st.get_sum(1, 3))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass SegmentTree {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; arr, tree;\n    int n;\n    int build(int ss, int se, int si) {\n        if (ss == se) return tree[si] = arr[ss];\n        int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n        return tree[si] = build(ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + build(mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n    }\n    int getSum(int qs, int qe, int ss, int se, int si) const {\n        if (qe &lt; ss || qs &gt; se) return 0;\n        if (qs &lt;= ss &amp;&amp; se &lt;= qe) return tree[si];\n        int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n        return getSum(qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + getSum(qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n    }\n    void update(int ss, int se, int i, int si, int diff) {\n        if (i &lt; ss || i &gt; se) return;                   // index not in this segment\n        tree[si] += diff;                               // this node's segment contains i\n        if (ss &lt; se) {\n            int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n            update(ss, mid, i, 2 * si + 1, diff);\n            update(mid + 1, se, i, 2 * si + 2, diff);\n        }\n    }\npublic:\n    SegmentTree(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; a) : arr(a), n(a.size()) {\n        int x = 1;\n        while (x &lt; n) x *= 2;\n        tree.assign(2 * x - 1, 0);\n        build(0, n - 1, 0);\n    }\n    int getSum(int qs, int qe) const { return getSum(qs, qe, 0, n - 1, 0); }\n    void update(int i, int newVal) {\n        int diff = newVal - arr[i];                     // how much every ancestor must change\n        arr[i] = newVal;\n        update(0, n - 1, i, 0, diff);\n    }\n    void print() const { cout &lt;&lt; \"tree:\"; for (int v : tree) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; v; cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    SegmentTree st({10, 20, 30, 40});\n    st.print();\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"sum(0, 2): \" &lt;&lt; st.getSum(0, 2) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"update(1, 60)\\n\";\n    st.update(1, 60);\n    st.print();\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"sum(0, 2): \" &lt;&lt; st.getSum(0, 2) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"sum(1, 3): \" &lt;&lt; st.getSum(1, 3) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class SegmentTree {\n        int[] arr, tree;\n        int n;\n        SegmentTree(int[] a) {\n            arr = a.clone(); n = a.length;\n            int x = 1;\n            while (x &lt; n) x *= 2;\n            tree = new int[2 * x - 1];\n            build(0, n - 1, 0);\n        }\n        int build(int ss, int se, int si) {\n            if (ss == se) return tree[si] = arr[ss];\n            int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n            return tree[si] = build(ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + build(mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n        }\n        int getSum(int qs, int qe) { return getSum(qs, qe, 0, n - 1, 0); }\n        int getSum(int qs, int qe, int ss, int se, int si) {\n            if (qe &lt; ss || qs &gt; se) return 0;\n            if (qs &lt;= ss &amp;&amp; se &lt;= qe) return tree[si];\n            int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n            return getSum(qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + getSum(qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n        }\n        void update(int i, int newVal) {\n            int diff = newVal - arr[i];                 // how much every ancestor must change\n            arr[i] = newVal;\n            update(0, n - 1, i, 0, diff);\n        }\n        void update(int ss, int se, int i, int si, int diff) {\n            if (i &lt; ss || i &gt; se) return;               // index not in this segment\n            tree[si] += diff;                           // this node's segment contains i\n            if (ss &lt; se) {\n                int mid = (ss + se) / 2;\n                update(ss, mid, i, 2 * si + 1, diff);\n                update(mid + 1, se, i, 2 * si + 2, diff);\n            }\n        }\n        String dump() { StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"tree:\"); for (int v : tree) sb.append(' ').append(v); return sb.toString(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SegmentTree st = new SegmentTree(new int[]{10, 20, 30, 40});\n        System.out.println(st.dump());\n        System.out.println(\"sum(0, 2): \" + st.getSum(0, 2));\n        System.out.println(\"update(1, 60)\");\n        st.update(1, 60);\n        System.out.println(st.dump());\n        System.out.println(\"sum(0, 2): \" + st.getSum(0, 2));\n        System.out.println(\"sum(1, 3): \" + st.getSum(1, 3));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class SegmentTree {\n    constructor(a) {\n        this.arr = [...a]; this.n = a.length;\n        let x = 1;\n        while (x &lt; this.n) x *= 2;\n        this.tree = new Array(2 * x - 1).fill(0);\n        this.build(0, this.n - 1, 0);\n    }\n    build(ss, se, si) {\n        if (ss === se) return (this.tree[si] = this.arr[ss]);\n        const mid = Math.floor((ss + se) / 2);\n        return (this.tree[si] = this.build(ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + this.build(mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2));\n    }\n    getSum(qs, qe, ss = 0, se = this.n - 1, si = 0) {\n        if (qe &lt; ss || qs &gt; se) return 0;\n        if (qs &lt;= ss &amp;&amp; se &lt;= qe) return this.tree[si];\n        const mid = Math.floor((ss + se) / 2);\n        return this.getSum(qs, qe, ss, mid, 2 * si + 1) + this.getSum(qs, qe, mid + 1, se, 2 * si + 2);\n    }\n    update(i, newVal) {\n        const diff = newVal - this.arr[i];              // how much every ancestor must change\n        this.arr[i] = newVal;\n        this.updateRec(0, this.n - 1, i, 0, diff);\n    }\n    updateRec(ss, se, i, si, diff) {\n        if (i &lt; ss || i &gt; se) return;                   // index not in this segment\n        this.tree[si] += diff;                          // this node's segment contains i\n        if (ss &lt; se) {\n            const mid = Math.floor((ss + se) / 2);\n            this.updateRec(ss, mid, i, 2 * si + 1, diff);\n            this.updateRec(mid + 1, se, i, 2 * si + 2, diff);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nconst st = new SegmentTree([10, 20, 30, 40]);\nconsole.log(\"tree:\", st.tree.join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"sum(0, 2):\", st.getSum(0, 2));\nconsole.log(\"update(1, 60)\");\nst.update(1, 60);\nconsole.log(\"tree:\", st.tree.join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"sum(0, 2):\", st.getSum(0, 2));\nconsole.log(\"sum(1, 3):\", st.getSum(1, 3));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>tree: 100 30 70 10 20 30 40\nsum(0, 2): 60\nupdate(1, 60)\ntree: 140 70 70 10 60 30 40\nsum(0, 2): 100\nsum(1, 3): 130</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(log n) per update</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(log n) recursion depth</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Min / max trees</div><p>For aggregates where a <em>difference</em> makes no sense (min, max, gcd) do not propagate <code>diff</code>; instead descend to the leaf, overwrite it, and on the way back up recompute each ancestor as <code>combine(tree[2si+1], tree[2si+2])</code>. That version works for every aggregate, including sum.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Forgetting to write <code>arr[i] = new_val</code> makes the <em>second</em> update of the same index compute a wrong <code>diff</code> — the tree then silently drifts away from the array.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Segment tree vs binary indexed tree — which one and why?</summary><div><p>Both give <code>O(log n)</code> point update and range query over a fixed-size array. A BIT is shorter to code and uses <code>n + 1</code> slots, but only works for <strong>invertible</strong> operations (sum, XOR) where <code>prefix(r) − prefix(l−1)</code> makes sense. A segment tree handles any associative operation (min, max, gcd), range updates with lazy propagation, and is easier to extend — at the cost of <code>≈ 4n</code> memory and more code.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is a range query O(log n) when it may recurse into both children?</summary><div><p>At every level at most <strong>two</strong> nodes are <em>partially</em> overlapped by the query (the ones containing its left and right boundaries); every other visited node is either fully inside or fully outside and returns immediately. So the recursion touches at most <code>4</code> nodes per level × <code>log n</code> levels.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How much memory does a segment tree need and why 4n?</summary><div><p>Exactly <code>2n − 1</code> nodes when <code>n</code> is a power of two; otherwise at most <code>2x − 1</code> where <code>x</code> is the next power of two above <code>n</code>. Since <code>x &lt; 2n</code>, <code>2x − 1 &lt; 4n</code>, which is why <code>4n</code> is the usual safe allocation. The iterative <em>bottom-up</em> segment tree needs only <code>2n</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you support range updates (add v to every element in [l, r])?</summary><div><p>With <strong>lazy propagation</strong>: store a pending <code>add</code> value in each node; a range update marks the fully covered nodes lazily instead of descending to every leaf, and the pending value is pushed down to the children only when a later query or update needs to look inside. Both operations stay <code>O(log n)</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can a segment tree be built for an array that grows?</summary><div><p>Not directly — the structure assumes a fixed size. Either allocate for the maximum size up front (unused leaves hold the identity element, <code>0</code> for sums), or use a dynamic / implicit segment tree that creates nodes on demand.</p></div></details></section>"}